{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"**Scope correction: it is FIVE call sites on current `origin/main`, not three or four.** The ticket names three and the follow-up comment adds the container writable layer as a fourth. `/var/tmp` is the fifth — `var_tmp_used_percent` (§2h, story `2ba3`) derives through the same helper. The complete set:\n\n| metric | cap | §  |\n|---|---|---|\n| `docker_storage_used_percent` | `DOCKER_BUDGET_BYTES` | 2f |\n| `docker_buildkit_cache_used_percent` | `DOCKER_BUILDKIT_CACHE_BYTES` | 2f |\n| `journal_used_percent` | `JOURNAL_MAX_USE_BYTES` | 2g |\n| `var_tmp_used_percent` | `VAR_TMP_MAX_BYTES` | 2h |\n| `container_writable_used_percent` | `CONTAINER_WRITABLE_BYTES` | 2i |\n\nThe defect spreading by ordinary reuse — three sites at filing, five a day later — is itself the argument for fixing the helper rather than the sites, which is what this change does.\n\n**Fix.** `pct_of_cap` is now `printf '%s\\n' \"$(( $1 * 100 / $2 ))\"` with no upper bound, and its comment states why a BUDGET ratio must be able to exceed 100 where a fill-level gauge need not.\n\n**RED before GREEN.** New `tests/scripts/test_observability_pct_of_cap_b380.py` drives the shipped function definition (lifted out by text — the script is a straight-line probe that cannot be sourced) and failed on the pre-fix tree with `assert 100 == 109` at the live production numbers, plus 150/300/1000 and a structural no-upper-bound guard. The four existing end-to-end clamp tests were rewritten to assert true ratios and each failed for the same reason first: journal `assert 100 == 200`, var_tmp `assert 100 == 225`, container `assert 100 == 300`, docker `assert 100 == 300`. All 65 tests across the five files pass after the fix.\n\n**Seeded-defect proof.** Re-introducing a clamp at ONE call site only (the BuildKit `--value` expression, leaving the shared helper honest) turns `test_a_budget_overrun_publishes_its_true_ratio` RED with `assert 100 == 240`. A future clamp re-introduced at any single site is caught. Reverted.\n\n**Consumer sweep — five alarms, no dashboards, one doc.** All five percentages are consumed by exactly one alarm each in `infra/terraform/monitoring_autodeploy.tf`: `rebar-docker-storage-cap-high` (407), `rebar-docker-buildkit-cache-high` (446), `rebar-journal-usage-high` (562), `rebar-var-tmp-usage-high` (655), `rebar-container-writable-usage-high` (750). **Every one is `GreaterThanThreshold` at `threshold = 85`, `statistic = \"Maximum\"`, `treat_missing_data = \"breaching\"`.** No alarm on any of these metrics uses `LessThanThreshold`, a band, an upper bound, anomaly detection or a math expression — the only `LessThanThreshold`/`threshold = 1` alarms in the file watch the 1/0 heartbeat metrics (`container_reaper_active`, `journal_cap_in_effect`, `var_tmp_cleanup_active`, `mcp_healthy`), none of which touch `pct_of_cap`. **No threshold and no unit needs changing:** a value that was clamped to 100 already breached 85, so every datapoint that fired before still fires, and unclamping strictly WIDENS what these alarms can distinguish rather than changing when they trip. CloudWatch's `Percent` unit is a label on a double, not a validated range, so 109 and 300 are accepted datapoints. There are **no `aws_cloudwatch_dashboard` resources in the repo at all**, so the \"rescales a shared axis\" argument in the original clamp comment had no actual consumer to protect. `infra/runbooks/review-bot-ops.md` gained a paragraph telling an operator that a reading above 100 is real and how far past the cap it means, and the BuildKit alarm row now reads \"at, or PAST, its own cap\".\n\n**S5 conflict.** Recorded as a comment on `910b-2d43-4482-4c64` (`francium-written-squid`) rather than by editing a closed ticket's description: AC3's \"percent clamped to 100\" is superseded, with the measurement that postdates it and a restatement of how AC3 should now be read. Its four other guarantees are untouched.\n\n**AC4 (re-measure live) is NOT satisfiable and is deliberately not claimed.** The reporting session pruned that build cache after filing; it reads 0 now, and this agent is barred from mutating the production host. The equivalent proof is the unit test at the exact recorded numbers (5 875 000 000 B against 5 GiB → 109), which is reproducible where the live host is not."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788617604013018201,"uuid":"176c44bc-0ba4-4038-8d7f-07368b06092c"}